A burst that overflows the C log ring is counted, and is over: what comes
after it still reaches the operator, and chatter nobody prints costs nothing.

stmak_log.h's ring is a bounded multi-producer queue: a producer takes a slot
only once it has seen the slot free, so a full ring drops the message it had
no room for and nothing else -- no position is left half-claimed for the
consumer to wait on or guess about.  Messages below the level any sink would
print are not enqueued at all, and messages below WARN stop taking slots at
three quarters full, so a burst of chatter leaves room for the line that
explains it.

The coating machine made the burst at every thread start: 23 EtherCAT slaves
going OP, HAL, motmod, DEBUG lines nobody prints -- more rtapi_print_msg
calls at RT priority than the ring held before the drain goroutine's next
poll.  With the earlier ring one such burst left a claimed-but-empty
position that the consumer waited on forever, and from then on faults
switched the machine off without a word.  ferror-message covers the delivery
chain and passes, because the sim never bursts.

logburst.comp makes the burst on demand: count messages at a chosen severity
in one servo cycle when its fire pin rises.  The test fires an INFO burst
larger than the ring, requires the drain to report the overflow with only
INFO dropped ("log ring full ... info=N ... error=0" in server.log -- proof
that the test overflowed, and that the headroom held), then opens a following
error the way ferror-message does and requires it on the operator channel
and in the log after the overflow report.  Then a DEBUG burst of the same
size, which the ring's floor must keep out entirely: no second report.
